# The Real Hyperbolic Plane as a Homogeneous Space

## Answer

**Both $SO(2,1)/SO(2)$ and $SL(2,\mathbb{R})/SO(2)$ are correct representations of $\mathbb{RH}^2$; they are isometric to each other.** The two quotients coincide because $SL(2,\mathbb{R})$ is the universal (double) cover of $SO^+(2,1)$, with covering kernel $\{\pm I\}\subset SO(2)$. The only subtlety is that $SO(2,1)$ is disconnected, so one should use its identity component $SO^+(2,1)$ to obtain a *single* copy of $\mathbb{H}^2$.

---

## Proof

### Step 1: The hyperboloid model and $SO^+(2,1)/SO(2)$

Equip $\mathbb{R}^3$ with the Lorentzian form of signature $(2,1)$:
$$\langle x, y\rangle = -x_0 y_0 + x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2.$$
The **hyperboloid model** of the real hyperbolic plane is
$$\mathbb{H}^2 = \{x\in\mathbb{R}^{2,1} : \langle x,x\rangle = -1,\; x_0 > 0\},$$
with the Riemannian metric induced by restricting $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ to tangent spaces. This is a complete, simply connected surface of constant sectional curvature $-1$.

The identity component $SO^+(2,1)$ preserves the form $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ and the sign of $x_0$, hence acts on $\mathbb{H}^2$ by isometries. This action is **transitive**: any point $x\in\mathbb{H}^2$ can be mapped to the base point $e_0=(1,0,0)$ by a Lorentz boost. The **stabilizer** of $e_0$ consists of elements fixing the $x_0$-axis, i.e., rotations in the $(x_1,x_2)$-plane:
$$\operatorname{Stab}(e_0) = \left\{\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&R\end{pmatrix} : R\in SO(2)\right\}\cong SO(2).$$
Therefore
$$\boxed{SO^+(2,1)/SO(2)\cong\mathbb{H}^2.}$$

**Caveat on connectedness.** The full group $SO(2,1)$ has two connected components (the component not containing the identity includes orientation-reversing or time-reversing elements). Since $SO(2)\subset SO^+(2,1)$, the quotient $SO(2,1)/SO(2)$ is the disjoint union of two copies of $\mathbb{H}^2$. To represent a *single* hyperbolic plane, one must use $SO^+(2,1)$.

### Step 2: $SL(2,\mathbb{R})/SO(2)$ via the upper half-plane

$SL(2,\mathbb{R})$ acts on the **upper half-plane** $\mathfrak{H}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:\operatorname{Im}z>0\}$ by Möbius (fractional linear) transformations:
$$\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}\cdot z = \frac{az+b}{cz+d}.$$
This action is by isometries of the hyperbolic metric $ds^2=\frac{dx^2+dy^2}{y^2}$ (curvature $-1$), and is **transitive** (e.g., translations $z\mapsto z+b$ and dilations $z\mapsto az$ move any point to any other). The **stabilizer** of $i\in\mathfrak{H}$ is:
$$\operatorname{Stab}(i)=\left\{\begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\\sin\theta&\cos\theta\end{pmatrix}:\theta\in\mathbb{R}\right\}=SO(2).$$
Since $SL(2,\mathbb{R})$ is **connected**, the quotient is a single connected space:
$$\boxed{SL(2,\mathbb{R})/SO(2)\cong\mathbb{H}^2.}$$

### Step 3: The two quotients are isometric

**Lie algebra level.** The Lie algebras $\mathfrak{so}(2,1)$ and $\mathfrak{sl}(2,\mathbb{R})$ are **isomorphic**: both are the unique split real form of $\mathfrak{sl}(2,\mathbb{C})$, of dimension $3$. An explicit isomorphism sends the standard generators to each other, and under this isomorphism the maximal compact subalgebra $\mathfrak{so}(2)\subset\mathfrak{so}(2,1)$ corresponds to $\mathfrak{so}(2)\subset\mathfrak{sl}(2,\mathbb{R})$. Hence the associated symmetric spaces are identical at the infinitesimal level.

**Group level.** The adjoint representation gives a surjective homomorphism
$$\operatorname{Ad}: SL(2,\mathbb{R})\longrightarrow SO^+(2,1),$$
which is a **2-to-1 covering** with kernel $\ker(\operatorname{Ad})=\{\pm I\}$. Crucially, $-I\in SO(2)$ (it is rotation by $\pi$), so $\{\pm I\}\subset SO(2)$. Therefore the covering descends to a diffeomorphism (indeed an isometry) of quotients:
$$SL(2,\mathbb{R})/SO(2)\;\xrightarrow{\;\sim\;}\;SO^+(2,1)/SO(2),\qquad g\,SO(2)\longmapsto \operatorname{Ad}(g)\,SO(2).$$
This map is well-defined (since $\ker\operatorname{Ad}\subset SO(2)$), surjective, and injective. It preserves the Riemannian metrics because both arise from the same Killing-form construction on the isomorphic Lie algebra pair $(\mathfrak{sl}(2,\mathbb{R}),\mathfrak{so}(2))\cong(\mathfrak{so}(2,1),\mathfrak{so}(2))$.

### Step 4: Summary

| Quotient | Connected? | Isometric to $\mathbb{H}^2$? |
|---|---|---|
| $SL(2,\mathbb{R})/SO(2)$ | Yes | Yes (single copy) |
| $SO^+(2,1)/SO(2)$ | Yes | Yes (single copy) |
| $SO(2,1)/SO(2)$ (full group) | No (two components) | Two copies of $\mathbb{H}^2$ |

Both $SO(2,1)/SO(2)$ (understood via the identity component $SO^+(2,1)$) and $SL(2,\mathbb{R})/SO(2)$ are **correct and equivalent** representations of the real hyperbolic plane $\mathbb{RH}^2$. They are isometric via the adjoint covering $SL(2,\mathbb{R})\to SO^+(2,1)$, whose kernel $\{\pm I\}$ lies inside $SO(2)$. The equivalence is rooted in the Lie algebra isomorphism $\mathfrak{so}(2,1)\cong\mathfrak{sl}(2,\mathbb{R})$.

